        "original_id": "7e89c40f-6c62-5096-9ded-3f23e43b9e42",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Lise Meitner, Otto Hahn, Otto Robert Frisch, Rudolf Peierls\n- Concepts: nuclear fission, uranium fission, energy release, atomic bomb detonation\n- Events: discovery of nuclear fission, 1939 publication, 1940 bomb mechanism design\n\nFacts:\n- Otto Robert Frisch was an Austrian-British physicist who lived from 1 October 1904 to 22 September 1979.\n- Otto Hahn and Lise Meitner led the small group of scientists who first discovered nuclear fission in uranium after it absorbed an extra neutron, and the results were published in early 1939.\n- Lise Meitner and Otto Frisch understood that uranium fission, which splits the uranium nucleus into two smaller nuclei, must be accompanied by an enormous release of energy.\n- Otto Robert Frisch and Rudolf Peierls designed the first theoretical mechanism for the detonation of an atomic bomb in 1940.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
